{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>אַף חוֹבֵב עַמִּים וגו׳</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Bava Batra 8a): even though HaShem befriended the nations and exiled Israel among them, nevertheless ״כָּל קְדוֹשָׁיו בְּיָדֶךָ וְהֵם״ and so forth — for those who learn Torah are exempt from tribute, poll tax, and the road toll. And it appears that Scripture intends to give a reason for His going to Seir and Paran, which was to engrave upon them the wrath of HaShem on the day of judgment, when they will say ״you did not make it known to us.״ Therefore He preceded with the cause and said to them: ״Do you wish to receive the Torah?״ And the reading of the verse is as follows: even — that is, also — that which He befriended the nations, in that He went to invite them to the Torah [is included]." "And His saying ״כָּל קְדֹשָׁיו״ and so forth — this means, in the manner of what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yalkut Shim'oni, end of parashat Kedoshim) on the verse ״וָאַבְדִּיל אֶתְכֶם מִן הָעַמִּים״ (Leviticus 20:26): had He said ״וָאַבְדִּיל אֶת הָעַמִּים מִכֶּם״ [I separated the nations from you], the nations would have had no standing; and from His saying ״וָאַבְדִּיל אֶתְכֶם״ and so forth, we learn that if some from the nations come to convert, they are accepted as one Israelite. And His saying ״כָּל קְדֹשָׁיו״ means this distinction still applies to them — meaning, to convert: whoever comes to be sanctified from among them, ״בְּיָדֶךָ״ — meaning, he enters and is received under your wings (Yevamot 47b)." "<b>Or</b> the intention of the verse may be as follows: ״אַף חֹבֵב״ — meaning, one act of love remained for the nations, namely that all His holy ones are in your hand, as I have explained. And His saying ״וְהֵם״ — meaning, the people of Israel, who are the very core of the matter — have a distinction that sets them above the holy ones who come from the other nations: Israel ״תֻּכּוּ לְרַגְלֶךָ,״ meaning, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yevamot 103b), Israel who stood at Mount Sinai — their spiritual contamination ceased; and this was by virtue of being תֻּכּוּ — meaning, they were cleansed through the power of the wondrous utterance they heard, the words of Elohim. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Shabbat 88b) that their souls departed, as alluded to in the verse (Song of Songs 5:6) ״נַפְשִׁי יָצְאָה בְדַבְּרוֹ,״ and thereby their contamination ceased. This is not so, however, for converts, for even though they are accepted as one of the tribes of Israel, the contamination did not cease from them, as is known from their words (Zohar vol. 3, 14a)." "<b>And He concluded</b> by saying ״יִשָּׂא מִדַּבְּרוֹתֶיךָ,״ meaning, a term of dominion, in the manner of His saying (Psalms 47:4) ״יַדְבֵּר עַמִּים תַּחְתֵּינוּ.״ The intention here is that no dominion is placed over Israel except upon one who is of the seed of Israel and not from the family of converts, as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Yevamot 45a) from the verse (Deuteronomy 17:15) ״שׂוֹם תָּשִׂים עָלֶיךָ מֶלֶךְ״ — all appointments you set over yourself shall be only from among your brothers; and go and learn from the baraita (Sotah 41a) of King Agrippas, who when he read in the Holy Temple, his eyes streamed with tears, and the whole story. And this is His saying ״יִשָּׂא מִדַּבְּרוֹתֶיךָ״ — meaning, whoever is of the seed of the people of Israel can be elevated to the greatnesses that exist over Israel Your people. And His saying ״יִשָּׂא״ is in the singular, as they said (Sanhedrin 8a): one matter per generation, and not two matters." "<b>It further intends</b> by His saying ״יִשָּׂא מִדַּבְּרוֹתֶיךָ״ that this is the reason for ״תֻּכּוּ,״ in the manner of what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Shabbat 88a), that HaShem held the mountain over them like a cask to receive the Torah, and in the days of Haman they annulled the claim of the great disclaimer and upheld of their own will what they had accepted under duress — and I have explained the matter in its place (Exodus 19:5): for their refusal to receive the Torah to the point that it was necessary to overturn the mountain over them was on account of decrees of the Sages and their words and enactments, which were many, that they made to caution Israel and to station them in a place guarded from transgressing the word of HaShem. And this is His saying ״תֻּכּוּ לְרַגְלֶךָ״ — in order that ״יִשָּׂא מִדַּבְּרוֹתֶיךָ,״ a portion of the utterances they did not wish to receive, for regarding the totality of the Torah it is written straightforwardly (Exodus 24:7) ״כָּל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר הַשֵּׁם נַעֲשֶׂה וְנִשְׁמָע״ — rather, it was regarding a portion of the utterances He commanded them to receive as the Torah of the Sage; for this, ״תֻּכּוּ לְרַגְלֶךָ״ — that is, Mount Sinai, upon which HaShem stood, and that mountain HaShem held over them like a cask." "<b>Or</b> interpret ״מִדַּבְּרוֹתֶיךָ״ in the sense of conduct/governance — meaning the teachings of those who guide Israel, for they are the ones who enact ordinances, decrees, fences, and safeguards. And in the days of Mordecai, when they saw how a righteous person performs wonders, they accepted the teaching of a wise man as a wellspring of life." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend by saying ״יִשָּׂא מִדַּבְּרוֹתֶיךָ״ in the manner of what our Sages of blessed memory said (Shir HaShirim Rabbah 1:2), that every single utterance that went forth from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, would speak with each and every individual of Israel and say to him: ״Do you accept me upon yourself?״ And he would say to Him: ״Yes.״ He would kiss him, etc., and rise and be crowned upon his head. And that is what is said ״והם תכו וגו׳ ישא״ — each and every one of them lifted מִדַַּבְּרוֹתֶיךָ upon his head. And it was precise to say ״מִדַַּבְּרוֹתֶיךָ״ and did not say ״דִּבְּרוֹתֶיךָ״, because the words themselves would speak to them and say to each one: Do you accept me? etc., as stated." "And His saying <b>יִשָּׂא</b> — in the future tense, because after the incident of the Calf those crowns that they bore upon their heads — which were the utterances speaking to them — were stripped from them, and they removed their ornaments at Mount Horeb (Exodus 33:5), and Moses merited them (Shabbat 88b), as they said of blessed memory (Chagigah 15a): one who merits takes his portion and his fellow's portion in the Garden of Eden. And this is the sod of what is said (Exodus 33:8) ״וְהִבִּיטוּ אַחֲרֵי מֹשֶׁה,״ concerning which they said of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 110a) that they suspected him regarding another man's wife — meaning, it is the Torah that was betrothed to Israel, of which Moses took their entire portion. Therefore it says ״יִשָּׂא״ — that they are destined to return to them in the time to come. And our Sages of blessed memory further said (Siddur of R. Asher in the name of the Ari z״l) that on the day of Shabbat the crowns return to the head of Israel, and nothing remains for Moses except the portion that is his due. And therefore it is that we say on the day of Shabbat ״יִשְׂמַח מֹשֶׁה בְּמַתְּנַת חֶלְקוֹ״ — meaning, let him rejoice even though he has nothing but the gift of his portion, for he has already restored to Israel their portion in this life." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend in the manner of what our Sages of blessed memory said (Horayot 8b): ״אָנֹכִי״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״ we heard from the mouth of the Gevurah. And we reconcile this statement with the statement (Mekhilta Yitro) that all Ten Commandments were spoken in a single utterance — for HaShem said all of them in a single utterance, but in proportion to the capacity of the hearers, they could not receive from them except ״אָנֹכִי״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״; they heard them in mighty power in a single utterance and their souls departed from within them. And that is what is said ״תֻּכּוּ לְרַגְלֶךָ״ — their strength failed until their souls departed. The meaning of ״לִשָּׂא מִדַּבְּרוֹתֶיךָ״ is: a portion of the wondrous commandments, which are ״אָנֹכִי״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״; but the rest Moses commanded them, as our Sages of blessed memory said (ibid.). And this is what he concluded saying ״תּוֹרָה צִוָּה לָנוּ מֹשֶׁה״ etc. And you will find that all the commandments that HaShem commanded — whether positive commandments or negative commandments — are in specific detail six hundred and thirteen commandments. And one who reflects upon the word ״תּוֹרָה״ will find in it the numerical value of six hundred and eleven (Makkot 23b), together with the two that are alluded to in the adjacent phrase ״יִשָּׂא מִדַַּבְּרוֹתֶיךָ״ — behold they are six hundred and thirteen, and it is correct." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend in the manner of what our Sages of blessed memory said in tractate Megillah (Megillah 29a), regarding that incident where the angels wished to push away Rav Sheshet from the first assembly because of the Shekhinah, until he protested and said: Master of the Universe — one who is insulted and one who is not insulted, who yields to whom? And HaShem said to them: Leave him be. And that is what is said ״וְהֵם תֻּכּוּ״ — meaning, when they are in a state of descent, insulted and afflicted and coming with this argument, He removes from Himself מִדַַּבְּרוֹתֶיךָ — which are the angels who stand to minister before HaShem, who are appointed over the matters of His Name's glory — and they are not pushed away on their account." "<b>Further,</b> he alludes in accordance with their words, may their memory be for a blessing (Berakhot 5a), that three good gifts the Holy One, blessed be He, gave to Israel, and all of them He gave only by means of suffering — they are Torah, and so forth. And they further said (Berakhot 63a) concerning the verse (Proverbs 30:32) ״אִם נָבַלְתָּ בְהִתְנַשֵּׂא״ — that whoever makes himself contemptible for matters of Torah, in the end he will be exalted. And that is the meaning of ״תֻּכּוּ לְרַגְלֶךָ״ — they afflicted themselves in this, ״יִשָּׂא״ — meaning, he will be exalted by virtue of Your words, for the Torah is what raises and lifts up, as it says (Proverbs 8:15) ״בִּי מְלָכִים יִמְלֹכוּ״ etc." "<b>Further,</b> it intends in saying ״תֻּכּוּ וְגוֹ׳ יִשָּׂא״ etc., in accordance with their words, may their memory be for a blessing (Nedarim 38a), who said concerning the verse ״וַתְּחַסְּרֵהוּ מְעַט מֵאֱלֹהִים״ (Psalms 8:6), that forty-nine gates of understanding were given to Moses at Sinai, while the fiftieth gate — is it not hidden with HaShem? And this is what I explained in the verse ״כִּי מָלְאָה הָאָרֶץ דֵּעָה אֶת ה׳״ (Isaiah 11:9) — meaning, the knowledge that is still אֶת HaShem, and He did not hand it over to Moses; and when our Messiah is revealed, HaShem will give that good. And that is the meaning of ״תֻּכּוּ לְרַגְלֶךָ״ — in the portion they already received; and in the future ״יִשָּׂא מִדַּבְּרוֹתֶיךָ״ — and He attributes to it the language of exaltation and preeminence, for that portion is exceedingly lofty; and we have already written about this at length in other places."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {7 "Resolving the Talmudic abbreviation הוריות as Horayot 8b, the Mekhilta citation as Mekhilta Yitro, and the Siddur of R. Asher reference per standard sources.", 8 "The comment is indexed 3 in the source. Resolving הוריות as Horayot 8b and the Mekhilta citation as Mekhilta Yitro per standard references."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:22:51.700640680Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}